  <div class="post-body">

    <p>My name is Ubaldo. Everyone calls me Baldo. I'm a barber from California with Mexican family roots, a Spanish tongue, and what my family politely calls "a travel problem."</p>

    <p>I've been to 22 countries. I've lived in Scotland, Mexico, Albania, and a few places in between that don't have great Wikipedia pages. I travel on a budget, eat where locals eat, and skip the tourist areas — not because I'm trying to be contrarian, but because the tourist areas are almost never where the real thing is.</p>

    <h2>Why the Blog?</h2>

    <p>Because most travel content is lying to you.</p>

    <p>Not on purpose, necessarily. But when you're reading a "best restaurants in [city]" roundup written by someone who spent four days there on a press trip, you're reading a simulation of travel. When you're watching a highlights reel on Instagram, you're watching a performance. The bad nights, the wrong turns, the places that didn't work out — those get cut.</p>

    <p>I cut barber hair for a living. I am not a professional travel writer. I don't have a media kit or a "link in bio" partnerships page (well, maybe a small one). What I have is a passport with a lot of stamps, a phone full of notes, and a genuine compulsion to tell people about the places that actually moved me.</p>

    <p>That's this blog. Real travel. Honest stories.</p>

    <h2>The Barber Thing</h2>

    <p>People always want to know how a barber ends up in Albania, or Colombia, or Fargo, North Dakota. The honest answer is: the same way anyone ends up anywhere interesting. You follow a feeling, you say yes to things that make no obvious sense, and you book the ticket before you can talk yourself out of it.</p>

    <p>Being a barber actually helps. It's a portable skill. Wherever you go, hair grows. I've cut hair in more cities than I can count. I've had conversations in barbershops around the world that I couldn't have had any other way — because the barbershop is one of the last places on earth where people still actually talk to each other.</p>

